B.aaf=new A.Z(B.cj6,["The perfect match: Sauvignon's boxwood notes and citrus freshness hug the richness of a Crottin de Chavignol or a Sainte-Maure goat's cheese.","A balanced pairing, based on the wine's aromatic profile.","Serve at the right temperature for the grape.","The noblest pairing of all: the melting texture of foie gras and the richness of a great sweet wine in perfect harmony.","Serve chilled between 8\xb0C and 10\xb0C.","A modern, lively pairing: the fine bubbles and freshness cut through the rich foie gras.","Serve in a chilled flute at 8-9\xb0C.","Gewurztraminer's spicy exuberance and roundness make lightly cooked foie gras shine.","The roundness and perfume of Alsace whites go well with lightly cooked foie gras.","Serve at 10\xb0C.","A connoisseur's pairing: an old Pinot Noir with melted tannins brings forest-floor and truffle notes that suit pan-fried foie gras.","Serve at room temperature, 15-16\xb0C.","The regional classic: mineral crispness and Alpine freshness (Jacqu\xe8re, Altesse, Savagnin) cut through melted cheese and help digestion.","Serve very cold at 8-10\xb0C.","A dry, taut white with good acidity cleans the palate after rich cheese and cured meats.","Serve at 9-11\xb0C.","A dry white is the ideal partner for melted cheese, keeping things light and moreish.","Serve chilled at 9-10\xb0C.","For red lovers at a raclette: this light, fruity, low-tannin red doesn't weigh the cheese down and goes well with cured meats.","Serve lightly chilled at 14-15\xb0C.","A legendary pairing: this Bordeaux's noble tannins and notes of cedar and black fruit lift tender roast lamb, while its toasty aromas answer the wild scent of thyme.","Open 1 hour ahead, without rough decanting (16-17\xb0C).","Decant for 2 hours to soften the young tannins (16-18\xb0C).","A textbook sommelier's pairing: sunny roundness, black pepper and southern garrigue and spice meet the thyme and roasting juices of a leg of lamb.","Serve at 16-17\xb0C in a large tulip glass.","The Iberian pairing par excellence: roast lamb with thyme loves the velvety tannins, leather and vanilla of this great Spanish red.","Serve at room temperature, 16-17\xb0C.","This structured red has the body and freshness to match rich lamb and herbs.","Serve at 16-17\xb0C.","This Bordeaux's noble tannic structure and cedar / blackcurrant notes are superb with the juices and marbling of the meat.","The sunny generosity and peppery, spicy notes of Syrah and Grenache stand up powerfully to grilled or roast meat.","Serve between 16\xb0C and 17\xb0C in a large tulip glass.","Mourv\xe8dre's breeding and its aromas of garrigue, leather and black fruit are masterful with meat grilled over vine cuttings.","Serve at 16-17\xb0C after a little air.","The wine's warm intensity, ripe tannins and toasty notes echo barbecue flavours and seared meat.","For a tender cut (beef fillet, pan-fried duck breast), Pinot Noir's silkiness and black cherry make a refined match.","Serve at 15-16\xb0C in a balloon glass.","This red has the freshness and structure to stand up to the dish.","A regional pairing in symbiosis: Pinot Noir in the sauce and in the glass gives remarkable aromatic continuity.","Serve at 16\xb0C in a large Burgundy glass.","A masterful earthy pairing: vigorous tannins and frank black fruit balance the richness of the beans and duck confit.","Serve at room temperature, 17\xb0C.","An aged red with notes of forest floor, truffle and leather melts into slow-cooked sauces and game.","Open gently without stirring up the sediment (16-17\xb0C).","This red's concentration and spice wrap around the deep flavours of the stew.","Decant for 1 hour to open up the bouquet.","A broad, round white beautifully wraps delicate meat and creamy sauces (morels, blanquette, poularde).","Serve at 11-13\xb0C, not too cold.","This white's roundness gently matches tender white meat.","Serve at 10-12\xb0C.","Silky tannins and crunchy fruit (raspberry, cherry) flatter roast veal or Sunday chicken without taking over.","Serve cool at 15-16\xb0C.","A balanced, mellow red for roast poultry.","Serve at 15-16\xb0C.","Mineral purity, saline freshness and citrus tension lift the pearly flesh of the fish without ever smothering it.","Serve chilled at 9-11\xb0C.","The wine's roundness and satiny texture echo a beurre blanc or a pan-fried sole meuni\xe8re.","Serve at 11-12\xb0C.","Freshness and balance that respect delicate sea flavours.","Serve well chilled (8-10\xb0C).","Creamy fizz and straight-laced bubbles lift fine fish in beurre blanc.","Serve at 8-10\xb0C.","The wine's sharp freshness and lime notes are wonderful with the acidity of the ceviche marinade.","Mineral precision cuts the rich fat of raw salmon or tuna while respecting soy and wasabi.","This white has the freshness to balance oily fish.","A food-friendly ros\xe9 is the ideal compromise between briny freshness and fruity roundness with sushi and seared tuna.","Serve at 9-10\xb0C.","A bold pairing sommeliers love: Pinot Noir's suppleness and fresh red fruit with a bluefin tuna steak or grilled salmon.","Serve slightly chilled at 14\xb0C.","The ultimate sea pairing: bright salinity and briny freshness are divine with oysters.","Serve very cold, at 8-10\xb0C, straight away.","Sharp freshness and citrus notes bring lovely freshness to oysters.","Thirst-quenching freshness, ideal with a seafood platter.","This fine white's texture and minerality lift the briny sweetness of shellfish and scallops.","Elegance and balance that respect the fine texture of shellfish.","Pure, lively bubbles wake up the palate and flatter lobster and oysters.","Serve at 8-9\xb0C in a tulip glass.","A legendary contrast: the salt and bite of blue cheese are magnified by the luscious sweetness of a great sweet white.","A sweet red, or a powerful spicy one, stands up to a strong blue cheese.","Serve at 15\xb0C.","The perfect match: this white's citrus freshness hugs the richness of a Crottin de Chavignol or a Sainte-Maure goat's cheese.","The white's freshness respects the cheese without leaving any bitterness.","Mild sheep's cheese with black cherry jam is wonderful with this red.","Serve at 16\xb0C.","The ultimate match for Comt\xe9 or Beaufort: hazelnut, butter and the minerality of a fine food wine in sublime harmony.","Serve at 11-13\xb0C.","Fine dry whites are the best partners for pressed cheeses, bringing out all their complexity.","A delicate, low-tannin red such as Pinot Noir respects the creaminess of Saint-Nectaire or Brie de Meaux.","This red's forest-floor and truffle notes resonate beautifully with mushrooms.","Serve at 15-16\xb0C in a large balloon glass.","A great mature Bordeaux shows notes of cedar and black truffle that are ideal with this dish.","Open carefully and serve at 16-17\xb0C.","The red's elegance suits the meaty texture of mushrooms.","Serve at room temperature, 16\xb0C.","The richness and dried-fruit / toasted-hazelnut notes of a fine food white lift a morel or porcini risotto.","Serve at 12-13\xb0C.","The bright freshness of a dry white or a Mediterranean ros\xe9 balances briny clams or creamy burrata.","Natural acidity and aromas of cherry and Mediterranean herbs cut through melted cheese and tomato sauce.","A friendly, generous red, perfect with Italian flavours.","The unmissable Alsace tradition: the sharp precision of Riesling or Pinot Blanc cleans up rich pork and sauerkraut.","The easy freshness and crunchy fruit of Gamay or Loire Cabernet Franc are wonderful with black pudding and apples.","Serve cool at 14-15\xb0C.","A structured dry white brings the acidity rich traditional dishes need.","This aromatic white's fruit and silky texture tame the heat and flatter curries and tagines.","A ros\xe9 brings welcome freshness to spicy dishes and couscous; the more vinous it is (Tavel, Bandol), the better it holds up.","Rounded tannins and southern notes of garrigue and black pepper round out lamb and North African spices.","An exceptional sommelier's pairing: the caramelised sweetness of hoisin and crispy duck skin love the silky fruit of this light red.","An Alsace white, especially with a touch of residual sugar, makes the sweet spices and duck glaze sparkle.","Chiselled minerality and bright white fruit answer fresh herbs (coriander, mint), ginger and lemongrass.","A crisp dry white to refresh you alongside wok cooking.","Serve at 9\xb0C.","A fruity, easy-drinking ros\xe9 brings lightness and fun to dim sum and stir-fried noodles.","The aperitif par excellence: fine bubbles sharpen the appetite and contrast with melting goug\xe8res and fine charcuterie.","Serve chilled at 8-9\xb0C.","The soul of the summer aperitif: a fresh, floral, crunchy ros\xe9 lifts tapenade and tapas.","A light, fruity red or a fine Spanish wine to show off the noble fat of Pata Negra ham and rillettes.","Serve at 14-16\xb0C.","A magical pairing: the cocoa, candied black cherry and prune of a Vin Doux Naturel or a Port lift intense dark chocolate.","Candied richness and notes of honey, dried apricot and vanilla are admirable with fruit tarts, tarte Tatin and cr\xe8me br\xfbl\xe9e.","Serve chilled at 8-10\xb0C.","Fine bubbles bring a light, festive finish to fresh-fruit or strawberry desserts.","Serve at 8-9\xb0C.","The great classic for a warm goat cheese salad: the citrus freshness of a Loire white answers the melting cheese and the dressing.","A crisp dry white keeps the salad light and respects the warm goat cheese.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 goes with the salad without weighing down the cheese.","A Ni\xe7oise salad calls for a dry ros\xe9, ideally from Provence: its saline freshness stands up to anchovy, tuna and olives.","A dry, saline white embraces the briny flavours of anchovy and tuna.","A fresh, rounded white answers the Caesar dressing, the Parmesan and the chicken.","Serve at 10-11\xb0C.","Bubbles lighten the creamy dressing and the Parmesan.","A light, fruity red served cool goes with bacon, gizzards or lentils without hardening under the vinaigrette.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 copes with vinaigrette and herbs.","A South-West red, served slightly cool, for the gizzards and duck breast of a P\xe9rigord salad.","A crisp, unoaked dry white, so as not to fight the vinaigrette.","An aromatic white, in the style of a Riesling or a Gewurztraminer, answers lime, coriander and chilli.","A fresh, dry wine without tannins, for the lime and chilli.","Failing an aromatic white, a very light red served cool.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 for feta, olives and sun-ripened tomatoes.","A lively, saline white answers the feta, olives and cucumber.","A crisp dry white respects the softness of the burrata and the acidity of the tomatoes.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 for ripe tomatoes, basil and mozzarella.","A lively dry white does not fear vinaigrette, the enemy of great wines: it answers it with its own acidity.","The bubbles of a brut keep the salad light and do not fear the vinaigrette.","Bouillabaisse calls for Provence: a dry, saline ros\xe9 or white from Bandol or Cassis answers the saffron, fennel and rouille.","A dry, saline white for the rockfish, saffron and garlic.","The buttery texture of a great Chardonnay embraces the velvety, briny sweetness of the bisque.","The fine bubbles of a Champagne lighten the cream of the bisque and lengthen its shellfish flavours.","A broad, fresh white goes with the briny sweetness of the bisque.","The Andalusian pairing: a dry, saline Fino or Manzanilla embraces the tomato, pepper and sherry vinegar.","Serve well chilled at 7-9\xb0C.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 for the tomato, cucumber and pepper of gazpacho.","A dry, lively white answers the tangy freshness of gazpacho.","A lively, straightforward white from Savoie or the Jura cuts through the grilled cheese and the sweetness of the onions.","A crisp dry white cuts through the grilled cheese and the sweetness of the slow-cooked onions.","A fruity Beaujolais served cool, as in Lyon's bouchons and Paris brasseries.","A rounded, buttery Chardonnay or Viognier embraces the velvety sweetness of squash or chestnut.","A rounded, fragrant Alsace white answers the spiced sweetness of the velout\xe9.","A rounded, supple white for the sweetness of the velout\xe9.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 for garden vegetables and basil.","A fruity, easy Italian red, in the style of a Chianti, for minestrone and its Parmesan.","A crisp dry white for garden vegetables and basil.","Garbure calls for a structured, straightforward South-West red, for the confit, ham and cabbage.","A straightforward, rustic red for a country soup.","An aromatic white, in the style of a Riesling, answers the fragrant broth, ginger and fresh herbs.","A fresh, dry wine without tannins, for the fragrant broth and herbs.","A fresh, dry wine without tannins goes with the soup without overpowering it.","Asparagus, so hard to pair, finds its wine in a Sauvignon or a dry Muscat, whose green notes answer it.","A dry, fragrant Alsace white answers the delicate bitterness of asparagus.","The bubbles of a brut go with asparagus without turning harsh.","A crisp, unoaked dry white, so as not to clash with the asparagus.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 for the chickpeas, sesame and herbs of mezze.","A crisp dry white for the lemon, sesame and herbs.","A rounded, buttery white, in the style of a Chardonnay, embraces the creamy sweetness of the gratin.","A rounded, supple white for the sweetness of the gratin.","A light, fruity red served cool to go with the gratin.","A fruity, lively Italian red answers the tomato, eggplant and Parmesan.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 for the tomato, eggplant and basil.","A fruity, supple southern red for the melting eggplant and the tomato.","A dry, fruity ros\xe9, as in Provence, for sun-ripened vegetables, olive oil and herbs.","A fruity, supple southern red with soft tannins, for slow-cooked vegetables and herbes de Provence.","A light, fruity red served cool, so as not to overpower the vegetables.","A broad, fragrant southern white for sun-ripened vegetables.","A crisp dry white for vegetables and herbs.","Eggs en meurette are poached in Burgundy red wine: a Pinot Noir from the same region is their natural match.","A fruity, supple red with light tannins, for the red wine sauce and the bacon.","The Alsace pairing: a dry, lively Riesling, Sylvaner or Pinot Blanc for the cream, onions and bacon.","A dry, lively white cuts through the cream and the sweetness of the onions.","A light Pinot Noir served cool, as in Alsace.","Quiche Lorraine finds its wine in Alsace: a dry, rounded Riesling or Pinot Blanc answers the cream and bacon.","A rounded, fresh Chardonnay answers the cream and egg.","Bubbles lighten the cream and egg.","A crisp dry white for eggs and herbs.","The bubbles of a Champagne, the brunch pairing, lighten the hollandaise and the runny yolk.","A rounded, fresh Chardonnay answers the hollandaise.","A dry, saline Muscadet or Loire white goes with a buckwheat galette, as in Brittany.","A crisp dry white for the buckwheat, ham and cheese.","A light, fruity red served cool, for the ham and melted cheese.","A dry, fruity ros\xe9 for the egg, potato and olive oil.","A crisp dry white for the egg, potato and olive oil.","A crisp dry white, in the style of a Chablis or a Sauvignon, for egg and herbs.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 goes with eggs without overpowering them.","The great classic with seafood: a lively, briny white, in the style of a Muscadet, a Chablis or a Picpoul, extends the taste of the sea.","The fine bubbles of a brut go freshly with seafood.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 for the griddle, garlic and saffron.","A crisp dry ros\xe9 goes simply with seafood.","A fruity, easy-drinking red, in the style of a Beaujolais or a Jura red, for the sausage, lentils or hachis.","A straightforward red for this hearty country dish.","A dry, straightforward white from Alsace or the Jura cuts through the richness of the cured meats."],t.w)
B.cj8={es:0,it:1}
