B.Ic=new A.Z(B.ciC,["Grapes & concentration","Grapes & fortification","Grapes","Sugar, acidity and candied-fruit aromas.","Concentrated fruit, sweetness and the warmth of alcohol.","The character of the grape, before ageing.","Bright gold with amber glints","Crystal-clear pale gold with a very fine bead of bubbles","A lively, creamy attack, silky fizz, and a long saline, chalky finish.","A lively, fruity attack, light bubbles, a fresh, thirst-quenching finish.","A lively, creamy attack, fine bubbles, a fresh and lasting finish.","Pear & white flowers","Tank-method fizz","Bubbles made in a tank, without long ageing on the lees, keep the wine's fresh fruit rather than brioche notes.","Warm brioche & toast","Yeast autolysis","While the wine rests on its lees in the bottle, the dead yeasts break down and release mannoproteins and amino acids.","A briny touch & fresh chalk","Cretaceous subsoil","The roots dig into the active limestone of Champagne chalk, bringing that inimitable saline freshness.","Tank-method bubbles","Charmat method \u2022 Fermentation esters","Why does this wine keep such fresh fruit?","The second fermentation happens in a sealed tank, under pressure, in a few weeks. The wine spends little time on its lees: it keeps the grape's crunchy fruit rather than the brioche notes of bottle-fermented wines.","Second fermentation & yeast autolysis","Mannoproteins \u2022 Amino acids","Why does Champagne smell of brioche and toast?","Why can a sparkling wine smell of brioche?","The second fermentation in the bottle traps carbon dioxide at 5 to 6 bars of pressure. Over months on the lees, the yeasts autolyse and enrich the wine with amino acids and mannoproteins: hence the brioche notes and finer bubbles.","Chalk terroir & chiselled acidity","Tartaric acid \u2022 Calcium carbonate (CaCO3)","That feeling of mineral purity and sharp freshness.","The limestone subsoil regulates water and root temperature perfectly. It keeps acidity high, which lets the best cuv\xe9es age for a long time without heaviness.","From deep garnet (Port, Banyuls) to amber (tawny, rancio), depending on the style","Golden amber with copper glints","Deep, luminous gold","A warm, rich attack, the alcohol blended into the fruit, a long finish.","A luscious, rich attack, sweetness balanced by lovely acidity, a long finish of honey and apricot.","Walnut & almond","Yeast veil (flor)","Under a veil of yeast, the wine oxidises gently and picks up its walnut and almond notes.","Prune & cocoa","Fortification","Adding grape spirit stops the fermentation: the wine keeps some of the grape's sugar, hence its roundness and candied-fruit notes.","Honey & candied apricot","Concentrated grapes","Noble rot or drying on the vine: the grape loses its water, sugars and acids concentrate, and notes of honey and candied apricot appear.","Fortification & the balance of alcohol","The balance of sugar and acidity","Ethanol \u2022 Residual sugar","Glucose \u2022 Fructose \u2022 Tartaric acid","Why is this wine both sweet and warming?","Why isn't a great sweet wine cloying?","Grape spirit is added to the wine during or after fermentation. During, it stops it and keeps the grape's sugar (Port, Banyuls); after, the wine stays dry (Sherry). The higher alcohol mellows with time.","A sweet wine can hold over 100 grams of sugar per litre. What keeps it fresh is its acidity: it balances the sugar and stretches the finish. It is also what lets the greatest ones age for decades.","Deep garnet with brick-coloured glints","Deep, dark purple with violet glints","A broad, fleshy attack, dense and structured tannins, a powerful finish of spice and fine oak.","Cracked black pepper & garrigue","Rotundone","Found in the skins of Syrah and Mourv\xe8dre, rotundone can be detected at just 16 nanograms per litre!","Blackcurrant & cedar","Cabernet Sauvignon gives blackcurrant and, with age, notes of cedar and pencil lead.","Ripe black fruit & sweet spice","Grape ripeness","Grapes picked fully ripe give these black-fruit aromas; time adds sweet spice.","Fine leather & damp forest floor","Tertiary development","Slow micro-oxidation polymerises the tannins and releases lactones and cigar-box aromas.","Bourbon vanilla & roasted cocoa","Oak ageing","Toasting the oak releases vanillin and smoky guaiacol into the wine.","Crunchy fruit & violet","Unoaked ageing","Without oak, nothing masks the fruit: it stays crunchy, and violet can appear.","Polyphenol extraction & tannins","Anthocyanins \u2022 Proanthocyanidins \u2022 Rotundone","Anthocyanins \u2022 Proanthocyanidins","Where do the dark colour and the grippy structure come from?","During maceration (punch-downs and pump-overs), alcohol extracts the anthocyanins (red pigments) and the tannins held in the skins and seeds. Tannins bind to the proteins in your saliva, creating that drying feeling which softens with time.","Oak ageing & barrel toast","Vanillin (C8H8O3) \u2022 Eugenol \u2022 Guaiacol","The alchemy between oak and wine.","For these wines, barrel ageing often lasts 12 to 24 months and brings gentle micro-oxygenation through the pores of the wood. Toasting the barrel caramelises the sugars in the oak, infusing vanillin (vanilla), eugenol (clove) and guaiacol (toast, coffee, cocoa).","Polymerisation & tertiary aromas","Ageing potential & softening tannins","Anthocyanin-tannin polymerisation \u2022 Ethers","Why does wine develop notes of forest floor and leather?","Why can this wine improve in the cellar?","Over years in bottle, tannin and anthocyanin molecules link into long chains (polymers). This softens bitterness and brings out tertiary aromas of forest floor, truffle, leather and tobacco.","Mature ruby with a translucent brick rim","Bright, clear ruby of medium intensity","A silky, lacy attack, tannins as fine as silk, a fresh balance and a light, saline finish.","Morello cherry & wild raspberry","Fermentation esters","A gentle, temperature-controlled fermentation keeps the very volatile fresh-fruit esters.","Faded rose petal & violet","\u03b2-damascenone & terpenes","Floral molecules typical of delicate reds, from Burgundy to the Loire.","A delicate grape & a gentle maceration","\u03b2-Damascenone \u2022 Ethyl esters","Why are Pinot Noir and Gamay so silky and light?","These grapes have thin skins, low in harsh tannins but full of floral and fruity aroma precursors. Whole-bunch or cold pre-fermentation maceration captures the purity of the fruit without extracting green bitterness.","The role of terroir","Drainage \u2022 Acid-base balance","That vertical, mineral feeling on the palate.","Poor soils (limestone, granite, schist) limit the vine's vigour. A steady supply of minerals supports a bright natural acidity that stretches the finish without any alcoholic heaviness.","Pale gold with sparkling green glints","A straight, chiselled, sharp attack, masterful saline tension, a vibrant finish of citrus and flint.","Flint & oyster shell","Kimmeridgian / terroir","Marine fossils (Exogyra virgula) in the marl reinforce the saline, briny impression.","Wet stone & citrus","Cool climate","A cool climate keeps the grape's acidity: that is where the tension and the wet-stone impression come from.","Pink grapefruit & boxwood","Varietal thiols","3-mercaptohexanol (3-MH) molecules, released by the yeasts during fermentation.","Lime & petrol","Riesling (TDN)","With age, Riesling develops TDN, a molecule with the petrol notes typical of the grape.","Citrus & white fruit","Cool fermentation","A low-temperature fermentation keeps delicate citrus and white-fruit aromas.","Aroma precursors","Glycosylated precursors \u2022 Esters","Where do a white wine's fruit aromas come from?","White grapes hold aroma precursors, bound to sugars or amino acids, that smell of nothing. A cool fermentation slowly releases them: that is where the citrus and white-fruit notes are born.","Varietal thiols & lively terpenes","3-Mercaptohexanol (3-MH) \u2022 Linalool","The secret of explosive citrus and tropical-fruit aromas.","White grapes hold aroma precursors bound to amino acids (cysteine). During a low-temperature fermentation, yeast enzymes break these bonds, releasing the volatile thiols behind the citrus and zest notes.","Salinity & the tension of malic/tartaric acid","Malic acid \u2022 Tartaric acid (C4H6O6)","Why does the wine make your mouth water so much?","In northern whites, malolactic fermentation is often blocked or partial to keep the sharp malic acid. That acidity directly stimulates the salivary glands and works as a natural flavour enhancer.","Bright, deep gold","A broad, luscious attack, rich texture coating the palate, a round and fruity finish.","A broad, rich, luscious attack coating the palate, lifted by fine oak and a vanilla finish.","Fresh butter & toasted hazelnut","Malolactic fermentation + lees stirring","Regular stirring of the lees enriches the wine with smooth mannoproteins.","Regular stirring of the lees in oak barrels enriches the wine with lipids and smooth mannoproteins.","Malolactic fermentation & diacetyl","Oenococcus oeni \u2022 Diacetyl (C4H6O2)","How does a white wine become buttery and velvety?","Lactic bacteria turn sharp malic acid into soft, creamy lactic acid. This produces diacetyl, the aroma compound that gives fresh butter its rich scent.","Ageing on fine lees & stirring","Mannoproteins \u2022 Oak lactones","Where does that rich, rounded feeling come from?","The fine lees are stirred back up from time to time with a wooden rod (b\xe2tonnage). As they break down, the yeast cell walls release large molecules that round out the acidity and naturally protect the wine from oxidation.","Clear, bright salmon pink","A crunchy, refreshing palate, balancing tangy fruit with a fine saline finish.","Redcurrant & grapefruit zest","Gentle direct pressing","A very short extraction limits contact between juice and skins, keeping only the delicate aromas.","Pneumatic pressing & temperature control","Free anthocyanins \u2022 Fermentation esters","Why is ros\xe9 pale and so expressive?","The grapes are pressed gently and cold, under inert gas, to avoid any browning. Only the first clear juice is kept, and fermented at 14-16\xb0C.","Look at the colour: hue, intensity, glints","Balance between acidity, sweetness, tannins and alcohol, then length on the palate.","Golden nose, seasoned taster \ud83c\udfc6 You spotted this bottle's key markers straight away.","Excellent sensory acuity \u2728 You picked up the wine's main components and its structure.","Good sensory intuition \ud83c\udf77 You caught some lovely traits of the wine; explore the subtler nuances below.","A promising exploration \ud83c\udf47 Sharpen your senses with the molecular secrets below.","Oak ageing (time in barrel)","Micro-oxygenation & vanillin","Softer, rounder tannins, and aromas of bourbon vanilla, toast, cocoa and clove.","Time in barrel does two key things:\n1. It softens the texture: the natural porosity of oak allows slow micro-oxygenation, which polymerises the tannins and makes them supple rather than harsh.\n2. It leaves an aromatic imprint: the cooper's toasting of the wood releases vanillin (vanilla), guaiacol (toasty, smoky notes) and eugenol (sweet spice).","Aged in stainless steel (no oak contact)","Pure fruit","Fresh fruit kept intact, undimmed freshness and a clear expression of terroir.","The temperature-controlled tank protects the wine from oxidation and adds no outside tannin, keeping the grape's primary aromas crystal-clear.","a length the appellation rules don't set","Length required by the appellation rules","The appellation's usual ageing","Appellation rules","Appellation custom","Stainless steel tank","Concrete tank","Oak barrel","Large oak cask","Concrete egg","Bottle (on laths)","Mourv\xe8dre (the king grape)","Structure & leather","Powerful tannic structure, deep notes of wild black fruit (blackberry, blueberry), fine leather, garrigue and forest floor.","The flagship grape of Bandol and the Mediterranean, Mourv\xe8dre ripens slowly facing the sea. Its very thick skin is full of polyphenols that give it a dense, chewy palate and exceptional ageing potential.","Carignan (a terroir grape)","Freshness & spice","Dark, bright colour, refreshing acidity, notes of tangy small black fruit, thyme and rosemary.","From old vines on schist or limestone, Carignan brings the acidity that balances the sunny power of southern wines, with a classy spicy backbone.","Syrah (an aromatic grape)","Colour & pepper (rotundone)","Dark colour with intense violet glints, signature aromas of cracked black pepper, fresh violet, tapenade and black fruit.","Syrah's skin concentrates rotundone, a highly aromatic molecule detectable from 16 nanograms/L that gives its inimitable peppery signature. It also brings violet anthocyanins and silky tannins.","Grenache (a grape of roundness)","Roundness & candied cherry","A round, velvety attack, generous warmth, seductive aromas of kirsch cherry, candied prune and cinnamon.","Full of natural sugars that turn into silky alcohol, Grenache rounds out the firmer tannins of its blending partners and brings the warm generosity typical of great Mediterranean reds.","Backbone & blackcurrant","A straight, firm tannic backbone, intense blackcurrant, cigar box (cedar) and minty freshness.","Its small, thick-skinned berries bring remarkable polyphenol concentration. It builds the wine's tannic backbone, which can last for decades.","Velvet & cocoa","A smooth, velvety texture, supple tannins from the start, charming aromas of ripe black plum, cherry and chocolate.","Ripening early on cool clay soils, Merlot brings flesh and generosity to the mid-palate, making the wine caressing.","Elegance & graphite","Airy finesse, crunchy raspberry and redcurrant, floral iris and a mineral touch of graphite / pencil lead.","The genetic parent of Cabernet Sauvignon and Merlot, it favours aromatic elegance and fine green freshness over sheer opulence.","Lace & morello cherry","Translucent ruby, ultra-fine lacy tannins, morello cherry, wild raspberry, faded rose and forest floor.","A very delicate grape: its thin skin gives silky tannins and a palette of floral aromas of rare purity.","Crunch & fresh fruit","Crunchy, juicy red fruit (strawberry, raspberry), peony and refreshing acidity without harsh tannins.","Often made by semi-carbonic maceration, it gives very moreish fresh-fruit aromas with low, easy tannins.","Density & blackberry","An almost black purple, a dense, fleshy palate, wild blackberry, violet and bitter cocoa.","Very rich in polyphenols, Malbec makes dark, tightly knit wines with great concentration.","Lightness & peach","Airy freshness, little astringency, notes of pomegranate, vine peach and rose petals.","A large-berried, lightly coloured grape, it lightens red blends and is the silky base of the finest Provence ros\xe9s.","Tension & green apple","Lively acidity, green apple, lemon, white flowers and chalky notes.","A chameleon grape: in a cool climate and without oak, it stays lean and lets the terroir speak.","Richness & brown butter","A broad, creamy body, golden apple, ripe citrus, fresh butter, toasted hazelnut and brioche.","The chameleon of winemaking: it takes beautifully to lees stirring and barrel ageing, gaining its rich opulence.","Tension & lively thiols","Sharp freshness, pink grapefruit, lime, boxwood, flint and blackcurrant bud.","Very rich in varietal thiols (3-mercaptohexanol), its aromas leap from the glass as soon as you swirl, with bright freshness.","Minerality & quince","Chiselled, vibrant acidity, chalky minerality, russet apple, ripe quince and acacia honey.","One of the noblest and most versatile white grapes in the world, its exceptional acidity lets the greatest age for a very long time.","Purity & precision","Crystalline purity, sharp acidity, lime, white flowers and fascinating mineral / petrol notes with age.","Riesling translates the soil's geology with surgical precision, never masked by new oak.","Apricot & opulence","A rich, enveloping texture, low acidity, a heady scent of ripe apricot, white peach and honeysuckle.","Its abundant terpenes show at full ripeness in the northern Rh\xf4ne (Condrieu), giving a floral, velvety palate.","Lychee & spice","An exuberant bouquet of lychee, rosewater, ginger, cinnamon and candied orange peel.","Its pink berries concentrate terpene and spicy precursors of an aromatic intensity unique among white grapes.","Red grapes & polyphenols","White grapes & terpenes","Grapes & gentle pressing","Varietal expression","Red or black fruit, tannic structure and purple glints.","Fruity freshness, mineral brightness and chiselled acidity.","Tangy freshness and a delicate salmon colour.","The grape's primary aromas are kept by a temperature-controlled fermentation.","Development & cellaring (tertiary aromas)","Tertiary bouquet","A garnet hue with brick tints, mellow aromas of forest floor, truffle, leather and tobacco.","Ageing in a sealed bottle brings slow polymerisation in a reductive environment. The primary fruit aromas turn into complex tertiary notes.","A subtle balance of sunny ripeness, sugar concentration and saline mineral freshness.","The soil (limestone, clay, pebbles or schist) regulates the vine's water supply, while sunshine and cool nights shape the freshness and aromatic signature of the wine."],t.w)
